.TH EJSON-ENV 1 "January 2026" "ejson" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ejson-env \- export environment variables from an encrypted ejson file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ejson env
[\fB\-\-keydir\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-key-from-stdin\fR]
[\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR]
[\fB\-\-trim-underscore-prefix\fR]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ejson env
decrypts the specified ejson, etoml, or eyaml file and outputs the contents
of the
.I environment
key as shell-compatible environment variable assignments.
.PP
This command is useful for injecting secrets into shell environments or
generating .env files for applications.
.PP
The output format is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export KEY='value'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Or with
.BR \-\-quiet :
.PP
.RS
.nf
KEY='value'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Values are properly quoted for shell safety, handling special characters
like single quotes, backslashes, and newlines.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-keydir= path
Path to directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Setting
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
will also set this value, with lower precedence.
.TP
.B \-\-key-from-stdin
Read the private key from standard input instead of looking it up in the keydir.
This is useful in environments where keys are provided via secure channels
rather than stored on disk.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the
.I export
keyword prefix. Output will be in the format
.I KEY='value'
instead of
.IR "export KEY='value'" .
This is useful for generating .env files.
.TP
.B \-\-trim-underscore-prefix
Strip the first leading underscore from variable names. For example,
.I _DATABASE_URL
becomes
.IR DATABASE_URL ,
and
.I __INTERNAL_KEY
becomes
.IR _INTERNAL_KEY .
This is useful when you want to mark certain keys as "exportable" in your
secrets file using a naming convention.
.SH FILE FORMAT
The input file must contain an
.I environment
key at the top level with a flat object of key-value pairs:
.PP
.B JSON (.ejson, .json):
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a...",
  "environment": {
    "DATABASE_URL": "postgres://user:pass@host/db",
    "API_KEY": "sk-secret-key"
  }
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B YAML (.eyaml, .eyml, .yaml, .yml):
.RS
.nf
_public_key: 63ccf05a...
environment:
  DATABASE_URL: postgres://user:pass@host/db
  API_KEY: sk-secret-key
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B TOML (.etoml, .toml):
.RS
.nf
_public_key = "63ccf05a..."

[environment]
DATABASE_URL = "postgres://user:pass@host/db"
API_KEY = "sk-secret-key"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only string values in the
.I environment
object are exported. Nested objects or non-string values are ignored.
.SH KEY VALIDATION
Environment variable names must be valid shell identifiers:
.IP \(bu 2
Must start with a letter (a-z, A-Z) or underscore (_)
.IP \(bu 2
May contain letters, digits (0-9), and underscores
.IP \(bu 2
Must not be empty
.PP
Keys that do not meet these requirements will cause an error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Basic usage:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson env secrets.ejson
export DATABASE_URL='postgres://localhost/myapp'
export API_KEY='sk-1234567890'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Source directly into current shell:
.RS
.nf
$ eval $(ejson env secrets.ejson)
$ echo $DATABASE_URL
postgres://localhost/myapp
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate a .env file:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson env -q secrets.ejson > .env
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use with Docker:
.RS
.nf
$ docker run --env-file <(ejson env -q secrets.ejson) myimage
.fi
.RE
.PP
Read key from stdin (useful in CI/CD):
.RS
.nf
$ echo "$EJSON_PRIVATE_KEY" | ejson env --key-from-stdin secrets.ejson
.fi
.RE
.PP
Strip underscore prefix from exported variable names:
.RS
.nf
$ ejson env --trim-underscore-prefix secrets.ejson
# _DATABASE_URL in file becomes DATABASE_URL in output
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred (file not found, decryption failed, invalid key names, etc.).
.SH SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
Decrypted secrets are written to stdout. Be careful about logging or
storing the output.
.IP \(bu 2
When using
.BR "eval $(ejson env ...)" ,
secrets become visible in process listings and shell history on some systems.
.IP \(bu 2
Consider using process substitution
.RB ( "<()" )
when possible to avoid writing secrets to disk.
.IP \(bu 2
Values containing null bytes will cause an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EJSON_KEYDIR
Directory containing private keys. Defaults to
.IR /opt/ejson/keys .
Can be overridden by the
.B \-\-keydir
option.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ejson (1),
.BR ejson (5),
.BR ejson\-encrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-decrypt (1),
.BR ejson\-keygen (1)
